# verse-dev drive: an agent's branch is a PROPOSAL, and somebody decides it.
#
# PLACEMENT: this belongs beside comment-drift.tony on the `use/verse-dev` branch, which is
# where a drive's charters live and what `origin` carries them for. It is here on main only
# because that is the branch it was written on, and putting it under `docs/working/verse-dev/`
# would create a directory that means one thing here and another there. Move it, do not copy.
#
# The coding runner delivers to `verse/auto/<task>` (coderunner/workspace.go: that prefix is
# chosen so a developer who pulls and finds a commit nobody wrote can tell which system made it
# from the first two segments). Today those branches accumulate and nothing consumes them —
# three on this repository, all abandoned. This is the other half: the branch becomes a fact,
# the fact carries a gate, and a yes merges it.
#
# TWO STRATA AND FOUR RULES, and the shape is borrowed wholesale from comment-drift.tony, which
# paid for it. `reflected` is that charter's — install it first, or move this into it; a second
# `verse stratum add reflected` is refused, which is the ordering saying the two share a floor.
#
# WHAT DECIDES THE DESIGN is staleness. A `verse/auto/*` branch is cut from whatever main was
# when its task started, so by the time anybody answers, main may have moved — and then
# `--ff-only` fails and something has to resolve a conflict nobody watched. The decision here is
# to REFUSE rather than resolve, and it is cheap precisely because the review that produced the
# branch is idempotent by construction: findings are facts, the ledger remembers, and a second
# pass reads its own memory and looks at what changed. Re-running a review costs a minute;
# rebasing an agent's work costs a second agent action and a second gate.
#
# THE MERGE RUNS NOW, AND THE SWEEP STILL DOES NOT. Both used to spawn a task that ran `git
# push`, and a task on the code runner could not reach a remote at all — which is what
# push-as-a-capability.md was written about and what has since landed.
#
# What landed: an environment declares DESTINATIONS by name, a task names one and never a ref,
# the vector gates the ref it resolves to, and approval is what releases the credential. So rule
# 2 below merges LOCALLY and then calls the runner's `push` tool naming `main`. Its prerequisite
# is in deploy/coderunner/coderunner.yaml, where `verse-fix` now declares that destination and
# offers the tool — a deliberate widening, and the comment there says what holds it and what to
# watch, since that environment is shared with comment-drift.
#
# What did not: rule 4 deletes a branch, and a delete is not something a destination expresses —
# there is no ref for it to resolve to. It stays unrunnable, and the shape it wants is a
# destination that permits a delete, which is a runner change rather than a charter one.
#
# `--ff-only` stays a prompt-level instruction on purpose, backed by
# `receive.denyNonFastForwards` at the receiving end: `where` scopes onto a credential and `how`
# does not, so a model that ignores the instruction gets refused by the remote rather than
# merging some other way. That refusal is now an ANSWER rather than an error — the task completes
# and says it could not, which is what rule 2's `onDone` branches on.
#
# That decision buys two things worth noticing. The merge ATTEMPT is the staleness test — no
# comparison operator is needed anywhere, because `git merge --ff-only` either works or does
# not, and rule 2's `onFailed` is what "it went stale under us" means. And the sweep below needs
# no clock: a branch's mergeability changes exactly when main moves, and main moving is already
# an event this verse reflects. A periodic check would be asking a question whose answer only
# changes on an edge we already have.

# Ours: one entity per delivered branch, and one for the sweep. Strictly above `reflected`,
# because that is the only direction a derive may go.
- name: merge
  level: 10
  match:
    system: merge
---
# 1. A delivered branch becomes a proposal, with the tip it was cut against RECORDED.
#
# `base` is the whole design. `!get-path` copies and does not track — read once, at the commit
# that caused the firing — which is exactly what is wanted here and would be a defect anywhere
# else: it freezes what main was when this branch appeared, so the proposal carries its own
# answer to "could this still fast-forward" without anything having to watch.
#
# It spawns nothing, so it needs no latch: a derive's address is a function of (rule, subject),
# so a re-fire writes the same bytes to the same address and the store ends it as a no-op. A
# force-push to the branch moves `sha`, re-fires this, and re-reads `base` — which is right, a
# redelivered branch is a new proposal.
- name: proposal-appeared
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject:
      system: git
      kind: ref
      value:
        name: !glob "verse/auto/*"
  action:
    derive:
      merge:
        proposal:
          repo: $subject.value.repo
          branch: $subject.value.name
          sha: $subject.value.sha
          base: !get-path { system: git, kind: head, id: verse, path: sha }
          state: open
---
# 2. …and somebody decides it.
#
# The gate carries a formula rather than waiting on a person indefinitely, and that is not
# ceremony: a question whose only discharge is somebody choosing to look is the wait
# `task/contract.go` property 5 forbids one level down, and it does not become acceptable by
# moving up a layer. `!until [!quorum 1, !deadline "24h"]` is the tutorial's documented shape and
# it defaults to DENY when the clock runs out, which is the safe direction for the one action in
# this repository with a consequence nobody can quietly undo.
#
# (A contract notice wants the INVERSE — carry on the clock, an objection interrupts — which is
# verse-dev step 2's open question. A merge is the case the documented form was written for, so
# it gets it, and the two genuinely differ rather than one being a bug.)
#
# `onDenied` is required by what a deny does: a refused firing writes nothing, so without this
# the subject is left in the exact state that asked, and the next commit re-poses the same
# question to somebody who already answered it.
#
# The task's own vector is `auto`. The decision has been made HERE, by a person, about the thing
# that matters; making the task re-ask per action would be asking the same question twice in two
# vocabularies.
- name: merge-proposal
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: proposal
      value:
        state: open
  gates:
  - name: merge-to-main
    mode: ask
    formula: !until [ !quorum 1, !deadline "24h" ]
    onDenied:
      patch:
        state: declined
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: runners:pool:code
      environment: verse-fix
      driver: llm
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        In ./verse, merge the branch $branch into your working branch and send it to main, and
        do nothing else.

            git -C verse fetch origin
            git -C verse merge --ff-only origin/$branch

        Then call the `push` tool with destination `main`. You cannot run `git push` yourself and
        must not try: you have no credential, and the runner is what holds one. It pushes what
        your branch is at, to the ref this environment declares as `main`, and it may be refused
        — by a supervisor, or by the remote's own rules about its history.

        `--ff-only` is not optional and must not be worked around. If it refuses, main has moved
        since this branch was cut and the branch is STALE: do not rebase it, do not merge it any
        other way, and do not resolve a conflict. Say you could not do it and why — a stale
        proposal is withdrawn and re-proposed by the review that made it, which remembers what
        it found and will look again at whatever main now says.

        A refused push means the same thing and gets the same answer.

        Answer with a JSON document and nothing else:

            {"merged": true, "branch": "$branch", "sha": "<the sha now on main>"}
      # `merged` is a RESULT and not a success flag, which is the difference this got wrong once.
      # Main moves constantly here, so a proposal is usually stale by the time anybody answers —
      # "could not fast-forward" is the ORDINARY outcome of this gate, not its error branch. The
      # task did its job when it tried and was refused, so it completes and SAYS so; failing it
      # would route the expected case through the branch meant for the unexpected one and lose
      # the difference between "main moved" and "the credential expired".
      #
      # task.Err draws the same line from the other side: "Err is infrastructure failure;
      # semantic failure is the charter's to check by naming what it expects". This is the
      # charter naming it.
      answer:
        merged: !irtype true
        reason: !irtype "not a fast-forward: main has moved since this branch was cut"
        branch: !irtype "verse/auto/example"
        sha: !irtype "9d007068dc826d6bd2df9e01f70bd601a2d33f7d"
    onDone:
      patch:
        # The branch on $data.merged is the shape this wants; the spelling is unverified against
        # the grammar and is the first thing to check when this is installed. Two rules
        # conditioned on the answer would say the same thing with no new operator.
        state: !when [ $data.merged, merged, stale ]
        mergedSha: $data.sha
        reason: $data.reason
        err: !delete null
    # Reserved for what it is for: the credential expired, the remote was unreachable, the task
    # broke. A refusal is not a failure and does not arrive here.
    onFailed:
      patch:
        state: failed
        err: $err
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        state: open
        err: !delete null
---
# 3. Main moved, so every open proposal's answer to "can this still land" may have changed.
#
# This is the "periodic" check, and it is level-triggered instead — because the thing it would
# poll for changes on an edge this verse already has. Width one: `git:head:verse` is ONE entity
# carrying main's tip, which is the lesson comment-drift.tony's wall TWO paid for with 131
# firings timing out inside the store.
#
# It spawns nothing, so it needs no latch, and `swept: pending` re-arms itself: a derive merges,
# so writing it puts the fact back in rule 4's selection every time the sha moves.
- name: sweep-armed
  stratum: reflected
  condition:
    subject: git:head:verse
  action:
    derive:
      merge:
        sweep:
          sha: $subject.value.sha
          swept: pending
          deleted: !delete null
          err: !delete null
---
# 4. …and the branches that can no longer land are deleted.
#
# Ungated, deliberately, and it is the one call here worth arguing with. Deleting a
# `verse/auto/*` branch destroys nothing that cannot be remade: the work was proposed by a review
# that is idempotent by construction, and a branch that cannot fast-forward is a proposal about a
# main that no longer exists. Against that, a gate on every stale branch fills the docket with
# questions whose only honest answer is yes, and a docket nobody can keep up with is one nobody
# reads — which is the failure the whole gate mechanism is for.
#
# What it must NOT do is delete a branch whose proposal is still open with a question standing on
# it. It cannot see that today; see merge-gate-residual.md, which is the shape of the ask.
- name: sweep
  stratum: merge
  condition:
    subject:
      system: merge
      kind: sweep
      value:
        swept: pending
  action:
    spawn:
      runner: runners:pool:code
      environment: verse-fix
      driver: llm
      grant: no-delegate
      vector: auto
      prompt: |
        In ./verse, retire the delivered branches that can no longer land.

            git -C verse fetch origin --prune

        For each remote branch under `verse/auto/`:

            git -C verse merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main <branch>

        A branch where that SUCCEEDS is still mergeable — main is an ancestor of it — so leave it
        alone. A branch where it FAILS cannot fast-forward onto main any more: main has moved
        past the point it was cut from, or it has already been merged. Both mean the same thing
        here, and both mean the branch is done:

            git -C verse push origin --delete <branch>

        Delete nothing outside `verse/auto/`. Do not merge, rebase or force-push anything.

        Answer with a JSON document and nothing else, listing what you removed — `[]` when
        every delivered branch can still land, which is the ordinary answer:

            {"deleted": ["verse/auto/some-task"], "kept": ["verse/auto/another"]}
      # `!irtype` reads only the TYPE of what it is written with, so a list-of-string exemplar
      # is how a list of strings is stated. NOT `!all !irtype "…"` — `!all` constrains every
      # element against a PATTERN and does not distribute over a scalar tag, so that spelling
      # parses, installs, and then rejects every well-formed answer with "expected !irtype
      # verse/auto/x, found [verse/auto/x]". Checked against answer.ParseSource before landing,
      # because a contract defect fails the FIRING after the work is done — one completed review
      # was lost that way (comment-drift.tony's note on `!all`).
      answer:
        deleted: !irtype ["verse/auto/some-task"]
        kept: !irtype ["verse/auto/another"]
    onDone:
      patch:
        swept: done
        deleted: $data.deleted
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        swept: failed
        err: $err
